we're going to start with some breaking  news two trains have collided in  southern pakistan's  sindh province killing more than 30  people according to local police  it appears an intercity passenger train  derailed and was hit by an  oncoming express service injured  passengers are being taken to nearby  hospitals  it's not yet clear what caused the  development and the subsequent  collision kamal haider is joining me now  from islamabad obviously we're in the  early stages  of uh this incident come up but what  else do we know  what we know so far is that this  particular  accident took place over 500 kilometers  from the city of karachi  in gothic district one train which was  northbound the other southbound collided  after one of the trains had a derailment  some of the bogies were lying  to the on the adjacent track when the  southbound train hit that with full  impact  according to reports 30 people are  confirmed dead that  that is according to the senior  superintendent of police in the area  rescue efforts were hampered and are  still being hampered because heavy  machinery is needed  to cut through the metal to get to those  people who may have survived  this terrible accident locals were the  first to respond there are  paramilitary forces at the site  however the heavy equipment has not  arrived and this is indeed a terrible  accident on a stretch of railroad  which is obsolete and there have been  other accidents on the same track  in the past as well come on just as  you've been talking to us we've been  looking at some of the pictures from the  scene  and we can see at least one of the  passenger carriages overturned possibly  one of the other carriages as well from  the other train unfortunately this is a  situation that we do  regularly report on at al jazeera just  talk us through  the safety record of pakistan's railways  well first of all it should be  remembered that most of pakistan and  india's railway system were set up by  the british  there was a modernization in the late  50s early 60s  but since then the railways has been hit  by rampant corruption  mismanagement and that of course has led  to an aging  obsolete system which should have been  basically  out of the system by now however despite  promises  of a new revamped railway line which  pakistan is negotiating with china  that plan of course has been hit by  financial constraints but the country  needs to do  a lot more in order to make railways  safer because this is  a mass transit a transport system  which caters mostly to the poor kamal  thanks very much indeed that's kamal  haider  bringing us up to date from islamabad
 

